WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.720
Maintaining flow is similar
to catching a current in the ocean.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.240
Once you're in it, you seem to be
gliding with the flow rather than

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.160
almost trying to hold on to it.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.680
It's something that just comes to you
and you stay with it.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.280
Flow is when a person is in
a highly focussed mental state

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.120
where nothing else seems to matter.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.040
It occurs when
your perceived skill level

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.960
and the challenge at hand are equal,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.760
and you are as in control of your
mind and body as you can be.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.120
When I experience flow I just feel
really, really focussed

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.560
and everything seems to works for me.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.240
You just feel confident
in everything you do

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.560
there's no negative
thoughts coming in

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.560
so you just feel like you can perform
to your best each time.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.080
The key aspects of flow

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.640
are being able to perform a task
seemingly automatically,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.600
having absolute concentration
on the task at hand,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.400
maintaining a sense of control
over the performance

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.560
and a loss of self-consciousness.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.440
In tennis there can be tons of things
that can distract you.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.760
When it comes to sport, many athletes
focus on the outcome of the game

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.200
the match or the race,
the desire to win,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.480
or they become distracted
by how they played in the last point.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.480
When athletes dwell on these
egocentric thoughts

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.720
we'll often see them
start to lose focus

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.840
and their performance
starts to deteriorate.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.280
The most difficult thing
about staying in flow

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.880
is the external things.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.840
Your opponent is obviously... Can be
one of the most distracting things,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.120
and just to isolate yourself
sometimes from your opponent

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.200
and simply focus on what you're doing

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.640
really helps for more focus
and flow display.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.200
Although being in a state of flow is
something that's usually associated

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.480
with high level athletes,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.080
it's something that potentially
anyone is able to achieve.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.600
From playing an instrument

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.240
to a surgeon carrying out
a complex operation.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.920
But what can you do to give yourself
the best possible chance

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.280
for achieving a state of flow?

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.400
Here are my tips to help increase
the probability of flow

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.680
in your everyday life.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.000
Sports psychologist Bob Rotella

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.240
highlighted the importance of letting
go of memories of shots, good or bad,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.840
and focus on the here and now,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.880
as it really helps you focus
on the process

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.680
and what you're specifically doing
at that moment in time.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.080
This could be achieved through
setting mastery orientated goals,

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.880
and you can recite to yourself
at times throughout the performance.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.360
A key characteristic of flow
is immediate feedback.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.160
This can be provided by teammates,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.760
coaches or yourself
as you complete the activity.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.280
When possible, try to figure out
what you're doing well and why.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.280
As well as what you can
be doing better and how.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.920
This will make you an active learner

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.480
and aid the development
of future goals and targets

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.920
necessary to achieve
your desired outcome.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.000
Be a better practitioner
of your skills, not a goal chaser.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.160
This feedback can be based on your
pre-set mastery orientated goals.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.000
Am I achieving these or not?
What can I do to achieve these?

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.560
Doing this as quickly as possible

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.040
will help you effectively move
on from situations,

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.640
and focus on the present.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.800
If the task is too easy then focus
is extremely difficult to achieve

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.080
and it may become tedious.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.680
If the task is too difficult

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.720
you may feel anxious about whether
or not you can meet the demands.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.360
Make sure you always set yourself
goals that match your skill level,

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.520
but also challenge you.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.120
When you achieve these goals,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.600
set yourself more challenging
targets in order to remain in flow.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.320
I personally find flow quite easy
to remain in but hard to find.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.960
But once you find it,
you're in the zone

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.960
an d, as it says, you just
flow through the match.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.260
Thanks for watching! :)

